#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205027} :activity/id #uuid "a89c210a-75b8-548a-8c6b-3cdd6c0b9acf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050832 :topic/id #uuid "6a24ddaa-5f01-58df-962b-9b8ee39de737" :topic/math-academy-id 5527} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205027 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205181 :tutorial/content "Recall that the gradient of a function \n\n$$\nf : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\ntells us how the function changes with respect to each of its input variables. It collects all the partial derivatives into a single vector:\n\n![](images/fe/fe9b4aa3c2e46dceeb10f63c021232f88ff1d3bd71f40fbd464b2ddb1cabef4a.svg) \n\nThis vector points in the direction of the *steepest increase* of the function, and its components describe how sensitive $f$ is to changes in each variable $x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n.$\n\nIn other words, $\\nabla f$ gives us a complete picture of how $f$ behaves locally around a point, not just in one direction, but in every direction.\n\nBut sometimes, we’re not interested in how the function behaves in every direction. Maybe some variables are being held fixed, or we just care about how the function responds to changes in a few specific inputs. In that case, we don’t need the full gradient. We only need the derivatives with respect to the variables we care about.\n\nThis idea leads us to the concept of the **subset gradient**, which captures how a function changes with respect to only a chosen group of variables while treating the others as constant.\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn what subset gradients are, and how to compute them. Let's look at a concrete example in the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "3b3c0bc1-1e2c-5a34-a144-c3d45e35be89" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11970 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d27dcae2-2914-5e30-a348-bffc446ad3dd" :step/math-academy-id 655778 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205028}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205182 :tutorial/content "A **subset gradient** is the vector formed by taking only the partial derivatives of a function with respect to a chosen subset of variables.\n\nLet’s look at an example to understand how a subset gradient works in practice.\n\nSuppose we have the function\n\n$$\nf(x, y, z) = x^2 y + y z^2.\n$$\n\nThis function depends on three variables, and its full gradient is\n\n![](images/82/823f3ea4fad73ab43a55bfc21aaacdfaf5a2c27cf69f77ce6d1fdac22af38175.svg)\n\nThe full gradient tells us how $f$ responds to changes in all three directions. But in some situations, we may only care about how $f$ changes when we vary a few of the variables, for example, just $x$ and $y.$\n\nIf that’s the case, we compute a subset gradient, which focuses only on the variables of interest. In this case, we compute\n\n![](images/33/3354ae8715103231292a40a2d042fb96ae29148c267f6960dad14e55fc2590f6.svg)\n\nHere, we are treating $z$ as a constant, as if it were fixed and not changing, while taking partial derivatives with respect to $x$ and $y$ only. This is the same idea that underlies partial derivatives more generally: we hold all other variables constant except the ones we are differentiating with respect to.\n\nSubset gradients are especially useful in problems where only some variables are being updated. They allow us to focus on the directions that matter for a particular step or subproblem, without being distracted by variables we are not actively changing." :tutorial/id #uuid "ba597b15-83d3-5390-b187-e0f31aa41102" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11971 :tutorial/title "The Subset Gradient"} :step/id #uuid "18068e19-7ad3-50e1-9409-20bce34116b6" :step/math-academy-id 655779 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205029}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204085 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203858 :question/id #uuid "aba1787c-af4b-5adc-8302-6302e891978b" :question/math-academy-id "e-17532"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efe27056-c688-56de-9859-98464f57ef64" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050143}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530474}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Basic Subset Gradients"} :step/id #uuid "45f1b211-fabf-5d85-b04d-98cf41d11be7" :step/math-academy-id 655780 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205030}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205030 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205183 :tutorial/content "So far, we have looked at functions with a few individual variables like $x$, $y$, and $z$. But in many real-world problems, we work with vectors of inputs and parameters rather than just a few named variables.\n\nLet’s return to a familiar example where we fit a straight line to some data.\n\nSuppose our *linear model* is given by \n\n$$\nf(x; m, b) = mx + b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $x$ is the input, and\n\n- $m$ and $b$ are the *parameters* we are trying to learn.\n\nIf we want to understand how the output changes when we adjust $m$ and $b$, we compute the gradient with respect to these parameters, denoted $\\nabla_{m,b} \\, f$. This tells us how sensitive the function is to changes in the slope and intercept, assuming $x$ is held fixed.\n\nThe same idea applies in higher dimensions.\n\nNow, suppose we have multiple input variables and define a model  \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}; \\mathbf{w}) = w_1 x_1 + w_2 x_2 + \\cdots + w_n x_n,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\mathbf{x} = (x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n)$ is the **input vector**, and\n\n- $\\mathbf{w} = (w_1, w_2, \\dots, w_n)$ is the **vector of parameters** (or weights).\n\nTo understand how $f$ depends on the parameters, we compute the *gradient* with respect to $\\mathbf{w}{:}$\n\n![](images/63/6334e1e6f6aa114a24cfaa586fe603f4bdfda8e0bffd118c7278b858bce527f7.svg)\n\nEach $x_j$ is treated as constant during this computation. Just like in the scalar case, we are asking how the output depends on each parameter while keeping the input fixed. This gradient gives exactly the information we need when updating model weights during training.\n\nNext, we will apply this result to a specific numerical example." :tutorial/id #uuid "77abc20b-1e21-53d4-848e-7f7ed772d03f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11972 :tutorial/title "Variable Groups"} :step/id #uuid "d7ded840-a03f-5738-9d0f-d4c11db692da" :step/math-academy-id 655783 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205031}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204086 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203859 :question/id #uuid "9599c265-6015-5ad4-b379-d72a67a1a475" :question/math-academy-id "e-17533"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6646d65-7c0f-56ae-995d-1de831893ab9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530518}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Parameter Subset Gradients"} :step/id #uuid "1d30b4ff-afd8-5008-b204-054f32819f05" :step/math-academy-id 655784 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205032}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204087 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203863 :question/id #uuid "91b597c2-12a1-5c93-93fb-dd9dd4a3a9fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-17704"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64331ffc-80a5-50c1-a7ac-d346458ce997" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530536}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Vector Subset Gradients"} :step/id #uuid "5ef0d644-c362-5795-a719-5177b39e1f96" :step/math-academy-id 655787 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205184 :tutorial/content "Just as we can compute gradients with respect to a subset of variables, we can also take second derivatives while focusing on just the variables that matter for our problem. \n\nWhen we write $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}}(\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}} f)$, we are computing all second-order partial derivatives of the function $f$ with respect to the variables in $\\mathbf{w}.$ The result is a square matrix where each entry describes how one component of the gradient changes when we vary one of the selected variables. This matrix is the **Hessian with respect to** $\\mathbf{w}.$\n\nTo make this concrete, suppose we have a function $f(x, y, z)$ that depends on three variables. If we only care about second derivatives with respect to $x$ and $y$, we write $\\nabla_{x,y}(\\nabla_{x,y} f).$\n\nThis gives us a $2 \\times 2$ matrix that contains the following second-order partial derivatives\n\n![](images/89/895686eb22d040a761ca4edfb8d5b65ca23c23cb06aadc63ce05be06a66d193c.svg)\n\nEven though $f$ depends on three variables, this matrix isolates the second-derivative behavior of $f$ in just the $x$ and $y$ directions. This allows us to analyze or optimize over part of the input space without involving every variable.\n\nLet’s go through a concrete example.  \n\nConsider the function\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}; \\mathbf{w}) = w_1^2 x_2^2 - w_2 x_1,\n$$\n\nand suppose we want to compute the Hessian with respect to $\\mathbf{w} = [w_1, \\, w_2]^T.$\n\nFirst, we compute the gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}^{2}{x}_{2}^{2}-{w}_{2}{x}_{1})=2{w}_{1}{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}^{2}{x}_{2}^{2}-{w}_{2}{x}_{1})=-{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}^{2}} & =2{x}_{2}^{2}, & \\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}∂{w}_{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}∂{w}_{1}} & =0 & \\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting everything into matrix form, we get the Hessian\n\n![](images/52/52bd7d2b21c9d884017376168f82fe4612157f0d92802d78e98d72b90f6f1658.svg)\n\nThis is the Hessian with respect to the parameter vector $\\mathbf{w}.$ It tells us how the gradient of the function (with respect to parameters) changes when we vary each parameter and reveals the local shape of the function near any given point." :tutorial/id #uuid "51d0646d-8c87-53c8-b1ae-0f819fe16145" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11973 :tutorial/title "Second-Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "686a637d-04aa-51c4-a7a8-d5a218313650" :step/math-academy-id 655790 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205034}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204088 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203860 :question/id #uuid "1639c64c-655b-5822-8464-0f4a704887df" :question/math-academy-id "e-17534"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26c6e982-dcf3-5521-84d9-5d8ca8b51a33" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048859}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530538}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second-Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "9dadb0c5-af81-5e65-9002-7529135fea83" :step/math-academy-id 655791}] :activity/title "Gradients With Respect to a Variable Subset" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204174}]}
